In the case that particles are produced from hard processes--either near the surface or throughout the entire volume--their spectra are expected to be well represented by a power-law function [1]. In Section 5.3 we use the power-law function

\[\frac{d^{3}n}{dp^{3}}\sim C(1+\frac{p_{T}}{p_{0}})^{-\alpha} \tag{5.5}\]

to parameterize the hard part of the \(K_{S}^{0}\) and \(\Lambda+\overline{\Lambda}\) spectrum.

### 5.3 Transverse Momentum Regimes

In what follows, we study the two component nature of the \(K_{S}^{0}\) and \(\Lambda+\overline{\Lambda}\)\(p_{T}\) spectra. We seek to delineate the boundary between the soft and hard region of the spectra.

